Object.assign(systemsDepth, {
  'apic-and-smp': {
    different: lesson('Combine two locally correct traces into one shared history', [
      'A single-CPU trace hides simultaneous ownership decisions because only one instruction stream executes at a time. On SMP, each CPU can make a reasonable decision from an observation that another CPU is about to invalidate. The combined history exposes the overlapping claims made by the two CPUs.',
      'Classify state before adding locks. Immutable tables can be read without mutation coordination after safe publication. Per-CPU state belongs to one processor under a stated access protocol. Shared mutable state requires an ownership and visibility rule. This classification is more useful than placing one giant lock around every function and hoping performance remains acceptable.',
    ], 'Allocate the final free frame on two CPUs', 'The allocator bitmap has one free frame F. CPU 0 and CPU 1 both request one frame while local interrupts are disabled on each CPU.', [
      ['Read the same state', 'CPU 0 sees F free. Before it records ownership, CPU 1 also sees F free. Disabling interrupts locally did not stop the other processor from reaching the same bitmap word.', 'both observations: F is free'],
      ['Produce duplicate ownership', 'Each CPU clears the same free bit and returns F to a different caller. The bitmap now says allocated, which looks reasonable, but two owners can write the same physical memory.', 'owner A and owner B both hold F'],
      ['Serialize the decision', 'Protect selection and reservation as one operation using the allocator’s shared synchronization protocol. One CPU obtains F; the other observes exhaustion or chooses a different frame after ownership is recorded.', 'exactly one allocation succeeds'],
    ], 'The allocation proof must cover the entire interval from checking availability to claiming ownership.', 'Protect shared state across processors', 'It can prevent local interrupt reentry but cannot exclude another core. Keeping the old uniprocessor masking pattern is useful only as one part of an SMP-safe ownership design.', 'What if each CPU has a private free list? That can reduce contention, but refilling and transferring frames between lists still require a shared ownership protocol so a frame never appears in two lists at once.'),
    apic: lesson('Trace an interrupt through routing and delivery separately', [
      'The source identity, route, destination CPU, vector, and handler are distinct choices. A device signal can be routed to a different vector from its legacy IRQ number, and firmware overrides can change assumptions about polarity or trigger mode. Keep the distinct source, route, and destination fields explicit in the routing record.',
      'Controller completion and device completion are also distinct. A level-triggered device can keep its line asserted until its service condition is resolved. Merely acknowledging the controller does not necessarily clear that device condition. The result can be an interrupt storm in which each completed delivery immediately causes another.',
    ], 'Route one device to a selected processor', 'A validated firmware topology describes an external source that the kernel chooses to deliver to CPU 1 at vector 0x51. Assume a level-triggered device for this trace.', [
      ['Install the route safely', 'Prepare the appropriate IDT handler and configure the selected controller route with the validated source, destination, polarity, and trigger settings. Avoid enabling delivery before its handler and device state are ready.', 'destination = CPU 1; vector = 0x51'],
      ['Service the source', 'CPU 1 receives the vector through its local interrupt machinery. The handler captures or clears the device’s pending condition using the device protocol. It does not assume that reading an unrelated status register completes the source.', 'device condition resolved'],
      ['Complete controller handling', 'Perform the required end-of-interrupt operation in the controller protocol, then return through the saved context. Repeated delivery after this point should prompt inspection of source state and route configuration.', 'handler returns to CPU 1 work'],
    ], 'Follow both the device and controller state to explain why the next interrupt occurs.', 'Do not leave duplicate legacy routes enabled', 'During migration from the PIC path, an event should not be delivered through two competing routes unintentionally. Make the transition explicit and test one source before enabling a full device set.', 'If vector 0x51 reaches the wrong CPU, is the IDT entry necessarily wrong? No. The IDT maps a delivered vector to code locally; the destination error can be in the controller routing or CPU identity mapping.'),
    startup: lesson('Publish online state only after private CPU state is ready', [
      'A topology table describes available hardware. Kernel initialization on each listed application processor requires a separate completion record. Maintain expected, starting, and online states separately. The scheduler may target only CPUs whose stack, descriptors, interrupt state, and per-CPU structures satisfy the kernel’s entry contract.',
      'The startup mailbox is another shared publication problem. Parameters must belong to the AP that consumes them, and the bootstrap CPU must not reuse mutable storage while a delayed AP still reads it. Starting APs serially simplifies this ownership proof, but a timeout still requires a policy for an AP that arrives late.',
    ], 'Start one AP while another never arrives', 'The bootstrap CPU expects APs with hardware IDs 2 and 5. It starts ID 2 first using a private stack and an acknowledged mailbox protocol.', [
      ['Initialize the first AP', 'Publish the selected startup parameters, issue the documented startup sequence, and wait within a bound. ID 2 establishes its private state and acknowledges that it is ready for ordinary kernel work.', 'ID 2: starting -> online'],
      ['Handle the missing AP', 'Attempt ID 5 and reach the wait bound without acknowledgment. Record a startup failure and keep ID 5 out of runnable CPU targets. Do not assume a table entry guarantees an active scheduler.', 'ID 5: expected, not online'],
      ['Preserve late-arrival safety', 'Keep or quarantine setup resources according to a protocol that prevents a delayed ID 5 from using freed stacks or repurposed mailbox data. Continue only with the CPUs that actually completed initialization.', 'schedulable CPUs = bootstrap + ID 2'],
    ], 'Online publication confirms that the processor has established every required initialization invariant.', 'Retain a private stack for each online CPU', 'Even if APs begin one at a time, their stacks remain live after they become online. Each active CPU needs storage that another startup attempt cannot overwrite.', 'Why is a timeout insufficient permission to free the AP stack? The processor may still arrive late. Resource release requires a protocol proving it cannot subsequently execute through the old startup state.'),
    atomics: lesson('Connect publication to the data it makes visible', [
      'An atomic ready flag prevents torn or racing access to the flag, but the payload requires an ordering relationship as well. A producer initializes the payload before release publication. A consumer reads the payload only after an acquire operation observes that publication. The protocol also needs a lifetime rule that prevents rewriting the object while the consumer uses it.',
      'For the first exercise, keep the slot one-shot: initialized once, published once, consumed without concurrent reuse. This excludes a second generation of data racing with the first reader. A reusable queue needs additional ownership state, sequence numbers, or acknowledgment; repeated reuse requires a proof covering each ownership transfer.',
    ], 'Publish a two-field message once', 'The shared object begins with ready zero. The producer will publish length 3 and payload bytes XYZ. There is one consumer and no concurrent reuse.', [
      ['Initialize privately', 'Write the length and all three payload bytes before the release store. At this point the consumer must still refrain from reading payload fields because readiness has not been observed.', 'length = 3; payload = XYZ'],
      ['Publish with release', 'Store ready = 1 using release semantics. This action orders the completed initialization before an appropriate acquire observation of the published value.', 'release ready = 1'],
      ['Consume after acquire', 'The consumer loads ready with acquire semantics and reads the object only when it observes the publication. It then sees the initialized fields under this protocol’s assumptions. A relaxed load alone does not supply the intended ordering argument.', 'acquire observes 1, then read'],
    ], 'The flag carries synchronization only when both sides participate in the agreed protocol.', 'PAUSE does not publish data', 'PAUSE is useful in a spin loop, but it does not replace acquire/release operations, provide ownership, or make ordinary racing C accesses valid. The same is true of merely marking the payload volatile.', 'Can the producer immediately overwrite XYZ after setting ready? No. The one-shot protocol gives it no evidence that the consumer finished. Reuse needs an explicit transfer of ownership back to the producer.'),
    tlb: lesson('Delay physical reuse until every old access is revoked', [
      'A mapping update changes page-table memory, while a TLB caches translations derived from that memory. The cached access can outlive the source entry unless the architecture’s invalidation protocol is applied. Frame lifetime therefore includes every CPU that may still use the old translation.',
      'The active CPU set is itself synchronized state. A CPU entering an address space during a shootdown must either join the current invalidation obligation or observe a generation that forces it to refresh before use. Otherwise a perfectly implemented list of acknowledgments can omit the processor that creates the stale access.',
    ], 'Unmap a shared address space safely', 'CPU 0 and CPU 1 have run the same address space. Virtual page 0x9000 maps to frame F, and CPU 1 may hold that translation in its TLB.', [
      ['Remove the table entry', 'CPU 0 changes the PTE under the address-space update protocol. New walks should no longer establish the old mapping, but F cannot yet return to the allocator because cached users may remain.', 'PTE absent; F still reserved'],
      ['Request and acknowledge invalidation', 'CPU 0 invalidates locally and requests the relevant action on CPU 1. CPU 1 performs its local invalidation and acknowledges completion using the agreed synchronization protocol.', 'wait for every required acknowledgment'],
      ['Release the old backing', 'Only after the protocol excludes all old accesses may F be returned to the PMM. A new process can then receive F without a stale write from the previous address space reaching its data.', 'F becomes reusable'],
    ], 'Transfer ownership only after every required revocation acknowledgment has arrived.', 'Waiting can deadlock the invalidation handler', 'Do not wait for a remote acknowledgment while holding a lock that the remote handler needs to make that acknowledgment. Also account for interrupt state and CPUs entering or leaving the address space.', 'What if CPU 1 never acknowledges before the diagnostic timeout? Report the failed revocation and retain or quarantine the frame. Reusing it anyway converts a responsiveness problem into a memory-isolation failure.'),
    scheduler: lesson('Move a runnable thread through one ownership transition', [
      'A runnable thread is a schedulable resource with exactly one owner at a time. It may be on one run queue or executing on one CPU, but it must not be both queued twice and eligible for simultaneous execution. Its saved stack and register context cannot support two processors resuming the same thread independently.',
      'Per-CPU queues reduce one source of contention but add migration and wakeup protocols. Define a consistent order for taking two queue locks, and protect the thread’s queue identity with the transition. Each optimization must preserve the established ownership rule under every permitted ordering.',
    ], 'Migrate a waiting runnable thread from CPU 0 to CPU 1', 'Thread T is runnable on CPU 0’s queue and is not currently executing. The balancing policy decides to move it to CPU 1.', [
      ['Acquire migration protection', 'Take the necessary queue and thread-state protection in a global order, such as increasing CPU index where appropriate to the design. Recheck that T is still eligible because another scheduler action may have changed it.', 'T owned by queue 0'],
      ['Transfer once', 'Remove T from queue 0, update its ownership metadata, and add it to queue 1 within the protected transition. There must be no observable state where both queues can independently dispatch it.', 'queue 0 -> queue 1'],
      ['Notify the destination', 'Release protection and send any required wakeup to CPU 1 if it is idle or must reconsider scheduling. Confirm T eventually runs once, with its own saved context and stack intact.', 'one execution owner'],
    ], 'Queue movement transfers the right to execute the thread to its new scheduler.', 'Measure cache traffic and protect ownership', 'Separating independent counters onto different cache lines can reduce false sharing after measurement. It does not make two unsynchronized writers to the same logical counter correct.', 'What if T wakes while migration is in progress? The wakeup path must use the same state protocol so it recognizes T’s current ownership and does not enqueue a second copy on its previous CPU.'),
  },
  capstone: {
    definition: lesson('Turn a feature list into falsifiable release claims', [
      'A capstone claim should name an input, a supported environment, an observable result, and a failure boundary. “Supports files” is too broad to test. “Reads fragmented short-name FAT16 files and rejects an out-of-range link without hanging” gives another engineer a concrete experiment and an honest limitation.',
      'Keep the acceptance record beside the exact artifact it describes. A source revision, toolchain version, build command, image hash, and emulator configuration identify what was tested. If any of them changes, rerun the affected claims against the changed image.',
    ], 'Write one verifiable process-isolation claim', 'The release targets the documented protected-mode machine and includes two test executables: a progress counter and a program that deliberately accesses a forbidden address.', [
      ['Specify the setup', 'Record the image hash, RAM size, storage configuration, and command used to boot. Start the counter first so it is an independent witness before the faulting process appears.', 'known image and configuration'],
      ['Specify the expected failure', 'Run the faulting process and require a diagnostic attributed to its identity and access. Require the offending process to terminate while the kernel and its healthy peer continue running.', 'one process fails'],
      ['Specify continued service', 'Require the counter to advance and a new valid shell command to complete in the same boot. Audit the terminated process’s owned resources so the claim includes cleanup as well as fault detection.', 'peer and shell survive'],
    ], 'The release claim becomes a small experiment another person can repeat without your narration.', 'Attach evidence to each completed milestone', 'Planned UEFI, SMP, or write-support work should have separate status and evidence. Completing the corresponding reading or helper checkpoint does not make those features part of the integrated release.', 'How would you narrow “supports FAT writes”? Name supported operations, naming rules, concurrency limits, persistence guarantees, and crash outcomes. Those constraints make the feature useful because a reviewer can tell what success actually means.'),
    testing: lesson('Place each failure at the cheapest layer that preserves its cause', [
      'A pure arithmetic bug does not require a full boot to reproduce, while a privilege-stack bug cannot be established by a hosted arithmetic test. Choose the lowest layer that still contains the mechanism responsible for the failure. This shortens feedback without confusing simplified evidence with complete execution.',
      'Use a pyramid of complementary observations: exact helper outputs, controlled dependency failures, and guest-level acceptance traces. The layers should share production logic where possible. Reimplementing the same algorithm in a fake can prove the fake correct while leaving the real caller untested.',
    ], 'Investigate a file read that hangs on a corrupt chain', 'A malformed image contains a cycle in a FAT chain. The kernel hangs while a shell utility attempts to read the file.', [
      ['Isolate link traversal', 'Reproduce the cycle using a bounded in-memory table and the production chain-walking logic. Assert a classified error within a fixed number of steps. This quickly tests visited-state or traversal-budget changes.', 'cycle: 4 -> 9 -> 4'],
      ['Add storage behavior', 'Run the same caller against a fake block device and inject a sector-read failure. Verify that I/O failure does not get mistaken for a usable link and that temporary buffers are released.', 'error propagates through real caller'],
      ['Restore guest integration', 'Boot the image in the selected emulator, run the utility, and require an error followed by a working prompt. Give the run a timeout and retain the last serial milestone if progress stops.', 'bounded guest failure; shell survives'],
    ], 'Each layer answers a different question about the same observed bug.', 'Equal totals can conceal wrong identities', 'A memory accounting helper can show that numerical categories sum correctly. It cannot prove that two categories contain disjoint physical frames. Add identity-level ownership checks when evaluating leaks or duplicate allocation.', 'Should every parser test boot QEMU? No. Most malformed byte windows can be tested directly and cheaply. Reserve emulator work for the integration properties that require privileged execution and real subsystem cooperation.'),
    zenos: lesson('Read source by following a value across boundaries', [
      'Choose one value to carry through the case study: a path, a byte offset, a cluster number, or a physical frame. At each call, write its unit, owner, valid range, and failure result. This produces an architectural explanation tied to specific calls, values, and ownership changes.',
      'Use the pinned revision linked by the chapter for reproducible source reading. Keep observations separate from proposed improvements. Inspect each caller’s bound and the helper’s actual comparisons to establish validation behavior. Likewise, an unfamiliar design may have constraints explained elsewhere in that revision.',
    ], 'Build a source-reading notebook for one file request', 'Take a command that asks to read byte offset 600 from a named file. The example is a reading method; it does not claim a particular ZenOS runtime result.', [
      ['Start at the command boundary', 'Locate how the command obtains the filename and requested position. Record which representation the next function receives and whether the input remains valid after the command handler returns.', 'value: file offset 600 bytes'],
      ['Follow storage translation', 'Inspect the VFS and filesystem calls that turn file position into cluster and sector work. Write the units at each transformation and find where the partition base is applied. Verify the responsible layer from its source and call sites.', 'bytes -> cluster span -> disk request'],
      ['Follow errors back upward', 'Choose one failed lower-level read and trace how its result reaches the command. Note whether output can be partial and whether the caller distinguishes EOF from error. Cite the inspected functions and revision in the notebook.', 'failure path reaches caller'],
    ], 'The finished artifact records the contracts found during source inspection; execution requires a separate run.', 'Do not silently switch revisions', 'Paths, signatures, and behavior may change on the default branch. Preserve the pinned revision for the case study, and clearly record a new revision if an optional investigation deliberately moves forward.', 'What evidence is needed before claiming a proposed fix works in ZenOS? Establish that revision’s native build, reproduce the counterexample, apply the change, and repeat the same experiment. Course helper results do not execute that separate repository.'),
    architecture: lesson('Label what each learning tool can actually observe', [
      'A diagram can show a modeled transition immediately because its state is deliberately small and visible. A focused machine test can observe registers or memory produced by compiled submitted code. A complete guest can reveal interactions among boot, interrupts, storage, and scheduling. These are different instruments, and selecting the right one is part of systems reasoning.',
      'When something fails, identify the instrument before interpreting the result. A compile diagnostic says the build did not produce the intended artifact. A machine timeout says execution did not reach the expected observation within its bound. Compare the model prediction with a separate observation of execution.',
    ], 'Study a page-permission failure at three levels', 'The learning question is whether a user access should fail when an upper paging entry forbids user access but the leaf appears permissive.', [
      ['Predict with the model', 'Change the relevant permission controls and trace the effective access decision. Explain why a leaf permission cannot override a restriction above it. The diagram makes the hidden intermediate reasoning visible.', 'prediction: access denied'],
      ['Test the focused helper', 'Submit the supported permission or range helper and inspect its expected and observed outputs. A pass shows the routine handles those supplied cases under the harness contract.', 'evidence: tested routine behavior'],
      ['Test the integrated kernel', 'Install actual page tables and execute the user access in the guest. Inspect the fault and continued kernel service. This adds evidence about CR3, entry stubs, fault handling, and cleanup that the helper did not establish.', 'evidence: cooperating machine path'],
    ], 'Confidence grows when explanation, focused execution, and integration agree within their stated scope.', 'A green indicator belongs to a specific artifact', 'Editing code changes the artifact under discussion. Previous verification must not automatically attach to the new source, even if the visible lesson and function name remain the same.', 'Which tool should demonstrate actual 64-bit SMP execution? Native QEMU with the advanced kernel and configuration. The selected browser machine and its conceptual models have explicitly narrower capabilities.'),
    compile: lesson('Treat source, build output, and verification as linked versions', [
      'A build consumes a fixed source snapshot, which identifies the artifact produced by that build. Record which source version produced the artifact and which artifact a test executed. This chain prevents an old successful run from being mistaken for proof about a newer edit.',
      'Stage-specific diagnostics help choose the next observation. A syntax error belongs to assembly or compilation. An unresolved symbol belongs to linking. A boot marker that never appears belongs to loading or execution after a build completed. Rebuilding repeatedly without classifying the stage wastes evidence already available in the report.',
    ], 'Edit while a build is still running', 'The editor contains source version A and starts a build. Before it finishes, the learner changes a comparison and creates version B.', [
      ['Capture the build input', 'The worker compiles the snapshot of A. Its output artifact is therefore tagged as derived from A even though the editor now displays B. The tool should preserve that distinction through linking and image creation.', 'building A; editor shows B'],
      ['Handle the completed output', 'When A’s build succeeds, it cannot silently verify B. The interface may discard it as stale or retain it with clear provenance, but current-source submission must use a build whose snapshot matches the current editor.', 'artifact A != verification of B'],
      ['Rebuild and execute B', 'Build the new snapshot, execute that artifact, and attach the observed results to B. If execution is canceled or times out, preserve the draft and report that outcome without reusing A’s earlier success.', 'B -> artifact B -> test result B'],
    ], 'Reproducibility begins with the exact source snapshot, even in an interactive browser.', 'Cancellation should not delete the project', 'Workers and guests are transient computation. The editable files and their saved draft state need independent ownership so stopping a runaway build or machine does not erase the learner’s work.', 'If linking fails, is a prior downloadable image current? No. It may remain a historical artifact, but its source identity must be explicit. A failed new build cannot make the old bytes represent the new project.'),
    pedagogy: lesson('Use a fresh counterexample to test your explanation', [
      'A solution is easier to remember when each condition has a reason. After inspecting a reference, name the simpler wrong approach and construct the smallest input that defeats it. This turns a line of code from something to imitate into a response to a demonstrated failure.',
      'A reflection answer should state assumptions, trace intermediate state, and explain the observed result. Keywords alone are weak evidence: a sentence can contain “overflow” and still perform the unsafe addition first. A useful rubric checks how each stated premise leads to the predicted result.',
    ], 'Explain a boundary fix without repeating its exact test', 'A range-check exercise taught you to compare a requested length with remaining capacity. Now transfer the same reasoning to a six-slot output buffer with four slots already used.', [
      ['Predict the legal remainder', 'Six minus four leaves two slots. A request for three output elements must fail before any of them are written. State whether rejection leaves output and metadata unchanged under this helper’s contract.', 'remaining slots = 2'],
      ['Construct the wrong approach', 'Imagine writing the first element, then discovering the third would exceed capacity. The partial modification violates an all-or-error contract even though the final check eventually detects the overflow.', 'late rejection is insufficient'],
      ['Explain the transfer', 'Preflight the whole request using the safe remaining-capacity comparison, then perform the writes. The representation changed from memory addresses to array slots, but the containment proof and publication discipline still apply.', 'prove first; publish afterward'],
    ], 'Understanding transfers when you can rebuild the argument for a new representation.', 'Reading an answer is one step in practice', 'After using Peek, close it and solve a changed case from the contract. Explain why your previous attempt failed, then compare behavior. Aim for a model that predicts fresh inputs and explains each boundary condition.', 'What should the final portfolio contain beyond source? Reproducible build instructions, diagrams with stated assumptions, valid traces, deliberate failures, and limits. Together they let another learner challenge and verify your explanation.'),
  },
});

Object.assign(systemsDepth, {
  'libc-and-shell': {
    boundary: lesson('Keep command policy outside privileged mechanisms', [
      'A shell is an ordinary client of process, descriptor, and filesystem services. Its command language is policy: how a user’s words choose programs and connect streams. The kernel’s role is to enforce the underlying operations regardless of which shell requested them. This separation makes the same services usable from a graphical launcher or another program.',
      'Built-ins are necessary when the requested change belongs to the shell’s own process state. An external program receives its own state or references under the process-creation contract. Changing that child’s working directory does not change the parent’s directory. The overwritten bytes were still needed as source data, so the defect follows from their overlapping lifetimes.',
    ], 'Trace a directory change followed by an external utility', 'The shell starts in /home. The user enters cd /docs and then show notes.txt. Assume show is a separately loaded ELF utility.', [
      ['Change the shell’s state', 'The built-in validates its argument through the filesystem interface and asks to change the shell process’s working directory. On failure it keeps the original directory and reports the error.', 'shell cwd = /docs'],
      ['Launch the utility', 'The shell resolves show, prepares argv and descriptors, and creates the child. Relative lookup of notes.txt uses the child’s inherited working-directory state according to the process contract.', 'child argv = show, notes.txt'],
      ['Return to the prompt', 'The child exits and the shell observes its status. The shell’s own working directory remains /docs. Loading and tearing down the utility should not require kernel knowledge of the command name.', 'shell remains available'],
    ], 'Mechanisms survive replacement of the user interface that combines them.', 'Putting parsing in the kernel enlarges the trusted surface', 'A quoting bug or malformed command should normally become a shell error. It should not require privileged code to interpret every command-language feature before a program can request ordinary file I/O.', 'What would happen if cd ran only as an external child? The child could change its own directory and exit, while the shell would still be in /home. The next relative path would therefore resolve in the wrong place.'),
    libc: lesson('Explain every compiler-visible effect of a syscall wrapper', [
      'Inline assembly has two audiences: the CPU executes the template, and the compiler reasons from the constraints and clobbers. If the template changes a register that the declaration describes as input-only, optimization can retain an invalid assumption. A wrapper is correct only when both descriptions agree.',
      'Keep a raw syscall layer separate from a friendlier library policy. The raw layer can return the course ABI’s signed result unchanged. A higher layer can translate errors into a documented C convention if you implement one. Mixing these responsibilities in arbitrary utilities makes negative-result handling inconsistent.',
    ], 'Move a small overlapping memory range correctly', 'A seven-byte array initially contains ABCDEFG. Move four bytes from positions 0..3 into positions 2..5 using memmove.', [
      ['Identify the overlap', 'The destination begins inside the source range and at a higher address. Copying forward would overwrite source bytes that later iterations still need, so this layout requires copying from the end toward the beginning.', 'source [0,4); destination [2,6)'],
      ['Preserve unread bytes', 'Copy D to position 5, C to 4, B to 3, and A to 2. Each source byte is consumed before a destination store could destroy it. The first two bytes and final G are unaffected.', 'result = ABABCDG'],
      ['Trace a destructive forward copy', 'A naive forward copy stores A at 2 and B at 3, then rereads the newly stored A and B after the original C and D have been overwritten. A dedicated overlap fixture exposes this even when every nonoverlapping memcpy test passes.', 'forward-copy result would be ABABABG'],
    ], 'Library function names promise behavior beyond merely copying some bytes.', 'State compiler and CPU ordering separately', 'The compiler’s memory clobber constrains its assumptions around the assembly. It does not by itself implement hardware synchronization, validate a user pointer, or establish cross-thread ownership.', 'Can memcpy be used for this overlapping example? No. Its nonoverlap precondition is violated. Use memmove or prove the ranges are disjoint before selecting memcpy.'),
    startup: lesson('Connect the linker’s entry symbol to the parent’s exit status', [
      'A C program’s familiar shape hides several contracts. The linker chooses an entry symbol, the loader installs its image and initial data, startup code adapts that initial state to a C call, and the exit path returns a status to the kernel. A working main function does not establish any of the surrounding pieces automatically.',
      'Keep the entry routine minimal and inspect its generated or assembled code. Establish the stack alignment selected by the target ABI before calling C, preserve the initial arguments while rearranging them, and avoid invoking library facilities that themselves depend on initialization not yet performed.',
    ], 'Run a utility that returns a nonzero status', 'A small program receives two arguments and returns 7 from main. Its parent shell waits for termination and prints the resulting status.', [
      ['Enter at the real start', 'The ELF entry names the startup routine _start. The loader provides the course-defined argc and argv layout. Startup code reads those values from user memory and adapts them to the chosen C call convention.', 'entry = _start; argc = 2'],
      ['Create a normal call', 'crt0 invokes main with the required alignment and arguments. CALL creates the return address that allows main to finish normally. The returned integer becomes input to the next stage.', 'main returns 7'],
      ['Exit through the process service', 'Startup code requests process exit with status 7. Kernel teardown releases resources and wakes the waiting parent. The shell reads the recorded process status to determine the command result.', 'parent observes status 7'],
    ], 'Startup and teardown turn an isolated C function into a complete process lifetime.', 'Check the required application ABI', 'An ELF binary may expect a different startup stack, dynamic loader, syscall set, or library ABI. Recognizing ELF32 does not make an arbitrary executable suitable for the course kernel.', 'What if main prints the expected message but the parent never resumes? Inspect exit, wait-state publication, and wakeup. Correct output proves the program ran that far; it does not establish a complete process lifecycle.'),
    io: lesson('Track the remaining slice after every partial result', [
      'A stream operation reports how many bytes it consumed from the supplied buffer. Maintain an offset into the buffer and a remaining count. After a positive result, advance both consistently. On error, report the documented partial-progress behavior; on zero progress for a nonempty write, stop or follow a documented bounded retry policy.',
      'The loop must also reject impossible results from its lower-level contract in a test harness or defensive wrapper. If an operation asked to write six bytes reports nine, blindly advancing would corrupt accounting. Real interfaces should guarantee results do not exceed the request, and fakes should help verify callers depend on that contract deliberately.',
    ], 'Write a fourteen-byte message in three pieces', 'The output buffer contains fourteen bytes. The underlying stream accepts four bytes, then three, then seven.', [
      ['Apply the first result', 'Start at offset zero with fourteen remaining. A result of four means only the prefix was committed. The next call begins at buffer + 4 and requests ten bytes.', 'offset = 4; remaining = 10'],
      ['Apply the second result', 'The next result is three, so seven bytes have now been written in total. Advance to buffer + 7 and request seven. Repeating the original pointer would duplicate the already accepted prefix.', 'offset = 7; remaining = 7'],
      ['Finish exactly', 'The final result of seven reaches offset fourteen and remaining zero. Return the wrapper’s documented complete-success result. No extra zero-length call is needed to discover completion.', 'offset = 14; remaining = 0'],
    ], 'The invariant offset plus remaining equals original length makes each step easy to audit.', 'Descriptors can share offsets', 'Two descriptor numbers may reference one open-file description. A read through one can advance the position observed by the other. Do not infer independent seek positions merely because the integers differ.', 'If the second call returns zero instead of three, should the loop retry unchanged forever? No. A nonempty write made no progress. Return a defined failure or use an explicitly bounded retry policy appropriate to that stream.'),
    parser: lesson('Record whether a word exists even when it has no characters', [
      'Tokenization needs more state than a character buffer. Track whether a word has begun separately from its current length. Entering quotes begins a word even if the quotes close immediately, so an empty quoted argument must survive as a real token. Whitespace outside a word remains a separator with no token to publish.',
      'Keep the language deliberately small at first. Define which escape sequences are recognized, where backslash is active, and whether an unmatched quote rejects the line or requests continuation. Do not accidentally claim full POSIX shell behavior from a tokenizer that only supports a few states.',
    ], 'Tokenize a command with an empty argument', 'Use the input: echo "" "two words". This teaching grammar supports double quotes to retain spaces and does not perform variable expansion.', [
      ['Finish the command word', 'Read echo in unquoted state. The following space completes the first token. Store its terminating zero and publish one argv pointer only after proving both output budgets have space.', 'argv[0] = echo'],
      ['Preserve the empty word', 'The next opening quote marks a word as started. Its immediate closing quote adds no characters, but the following separator still publishes a pointer to an empty zero-terminated string.', 'argv[1] = empty string'],
      ['Retain the internal space', 'Inside the final quotes, the space between two and words is ordinary content. Closing the quotes completes the third token. The result contains exactly three arguments.', 'argc = 3; argv[2] = two words'],
    ], 'A state transition can create meaning even when it emits no character.', 'One capacity check does not cover both outputs', 'A command with many short words can exhaust argv slots while character storage remains. One huge word can exhaust character storage first. Check each limit before its corresponding write.', 'What should happen after an unmatched final quote? Follow the documented syntax-error or continuation policy. Never treat the missing delimiter as permission to scan past the bounded input line.'),
    session: lesson('Choose a pipeline large enough to require cooperation', [
      'A tiny pipeline can finish entirely inside the pipe buffer before the consumer runs. That proves little about blocking or wakeups. Choose a payload larger than capacity and require both producer and consumer to make progress in the same boot. Record endpoint references as well as byte counts when diagnosing a hang.',
      'An integration session should end with another ordinary command. This tests whether cleanup preserved the shell, descriptor table, runnable queues, and memory ownership. Successful output followed by a permanently blocked prompt is an incomplete result even if the displayed file contents were correct.',
    ], 'Move ten bytes through a four-byte pipe', 'Producer P emits ABCDEFGHIJ. Consumer C requests three bytes per read. The pipe capacity is four and both endpoints use the blocking contract.', [
      ['Fill and block', 'P inserts ABCD and cannot insert another byte until space appears. The scheduler must let C run. An implementation that only busy-waits in P can prevent the consumer from creating the very condition P needs.', 'buffer full; P waits'],
      ['Alternate progress', 'C reads ABC, making room for P to add EFG. Later reads and writes continue from their updated offsets. The exact grouping may vary, but concatenated output must remain ABCDEFGHIJ with no duplication or loss.', 'logical byte order preserved'],
      ['Close and finish', 'P closes its final writer reference after its last byte. C drains the remainder, observes EOF, and exits. The parent closes unused endpoints, observes child results, and accepts a fresh command.', 'EOF after all ten bytes'],
    ], 'The oversized payload forces the scheduling and ownership paths the course is trying to teach.', 'Printed bytes do not prove final closure', 'A consumer can display every byte and still wait forever if an unused writer reference remains open. Include process completion and the returned prompt in the acceptance record.', 'What changes if C exits early? P must receive the documented no-reader outcome when the last reader closes. That case tests endpoint closure and wakeup from the producer’s side.'),
  },
  'long-mode-and-uefi': {
    separate: lesson('Change the loader contract and CPU mode independently', [
      'A migration is easier to debug when each stage changes one boundary. The firmware entry path determines what services and initial machine state the loader receives. The kernel execution mode determines instruction decoding, registers, and translation rules. A shared boot-information contract can connect multiple loaders to the same kernel without pretending those loaders execute identically.',
      'Give every boot-information address a unit, address space, and lifetime. After changing page tables, the kernel needs a virtual mapping for the recorded physical framebuffer. An array of memory descriptors cannot be reclaimed until the kernel has copied or adopted it. Version and size fields let a new kernel reject an incompatible handoff cleanly.',
    ], 'Preserve a boot module while replacing mappings', 'The loader places a module at physical 0x01200000 with length 0x18000 and records that physical span in boot information.', [
      ['Reserve its backing', 'Mark the module’s frames unavailable to general allocation until consumption finishes. With 4 KiB pages, 0x18000 bytes occupy 24 pages when the start is page-aligned.', 'reserved module pages = 24'],
      ['Install an explicit mapping', 'The kernel chooses a virtual window through which to inspect the module. It maps that virtual window explicitly to the recorded physical frames.', 'physical location != automatic pointer'],
      ['Transfer lifetime ownership', 'After copying or adopting the module data, release only storage no longer referenced. If the kernel retains pointers into the original module, the original frames must remain reserved.', 'release follows final use'],
    ], 'A stable handoff structure reduces the number of assumptions that change during a port.', 'Validate native execution separately', 'The course’s supported browser machine executes the existing 32-bit work. Use native QEMU and the appropriate firmware for actual long-mode or UEFI execution, while using the browser model to explain the planned transitions.', 'If the boot-information structure itself lies in reclaimable memory, when may the PMM reuse it? Only after every field and referenced object still needed has been copied, adopted, or separately reserved.'),
    'long-mode': lesson('Prove that the transition can fetch its next instruction', [
      'Changing control bits affects the interpretation of future addresses and instructions. The transition code therefore needs a reachability proof: its current location, the next transfer target, the stack, and any data consulted during entry must remain accessible under the newly active translation scheme. A page table that maps only the final kernel entry can strand the transition before it reaches that entry.',
      'Keep the initial assumptions narrow: the taught transition starts from protected mode with paging off, uses four levels and 4 KiB leaves, and enables supported features in the required sequence. A boot path arriving in a different state needs an adapted sequence. A valid mode switch requires each instruction’s prerequisites to hold in the current machine state.',
    ], 'Split a small virtual address into walk indices', 'Consider virtual address 0x0000000000403123 under four-level, 48-bit canonical addressing with 4 KiB pages.', [
      ['Separate the offset', 'The low twelve bits are 0x123, or 291 bytes into the final frame. Removing those bits leaves the page number used to select translation entries.', 'offset = 0x123'],
      ['Select each level', 'The PML4 index is 0, the PDPT index is 0, the PD index is 2, and the PT index is 3. Each index selects one of 512 entries in its own table.', 'indices = 0 / 0 / 2 / 3'],
      ['Apply mapping and permissions', 'If the leaf names frame 0x02000000 and all required entries permit the access, the physical byte is 0x02000123. A correct canonical shape and correct indices still do not prove that those entries are present.', 'physical = frame + offset'],
    ], 'Address shape, page-walk structure, and access permission remain separate checks.', 'Apply the selected canonical address rule', 'With the selected 48-bit canonical rule, upper bits must match bit 47. Validate high bits against that canonical rule before treating the stored integer as an address.', 'What is the next virtual page after this one? 0x0000000000404000. It keeps the first three indices and changes the PT index to 4, while its page offset resets to zero.'),
    abi: lesson('Audit every assembly-to-C boundary during the port', [
      'An ABI is executable agreement: argument placement, preserved registers, stack alignment, and data layout all affect whether separately compiled code cooperates. A port can compile cleanly while violating that agreement at the first call. Write a boundary table for entry stubs, interrupt handlers, ordinary C calls, and firmware calls before widening types.',
      'Generated offsets or compile-time assertions connect assembly layouts to C structures. This is especially valuable when a pointer grows but nearby fixed-width fields do not. Padding can move a field even when the names and apparent order remain unchanged. The instruction using an old offset will not know that the compiler chose a new layout.',
    ], 'Pass two kernel arguments under the selected 64-bit ABI', 'A System V AMD64 kernel function takes a frame pointer and an unsigned reason value. An assembly stub has captured both and is ready to call it.', [
      ['Place the arguments', 'Arrange the first integer-class argument in RDI and the second in RSI for this ordinary non-variadic call. Do not leave them only at the old i386 stack offsets and expect the new compiler to find them.', 'RDI = frame; RSI = reason'],
      ['Align the call boundary', 'Adjust the stack according to the selected ABI before CALL, accounting for every saved register and the return address it pushes. Keep the interrupt-frame pointer valid while any alignment padding changes RSP.', 'alignment tracked explicitly'],
      ['Restore the saved context', 'After C returns, restore exactly the registers and frame state promised by the interrupt-entry contract. Interrupt entry must preserve the complete state required to resume the interrupted program, including values outside the ordinary C preservation set.', 'return to the interrupted context'],
    ], 'The stub adapts two contracts: hardware entry and compiler-generated function calls.', 'Firmware uses its own calling convention', 'x64 UEFI calls need the specified firmware ABI, including its register and stack rules. A function-pointer cast does not create an adapter from the kernel’s System V convention.', 'Why disable the red zone for interrupt-capable kernel code? Asynchronous entry may use memory below the current stack pointer. Kernel entry must explicitly preserve any area that compiled code treats as a red zone.'),
    uefi: lesson('Make the final memory map and exit one narrow protocol', [
      'The memory-map key identifies the firmware state represented by a particular map observation. Allocating memory after obtaining it can change that state, so the exit call may reject the stale key. Prepare as much work as possible before the final map retrieval, including storage large enough to tolerate map growth.',
      'After a successful exit, the kernel owns the boot-services handoff and must not continue using boot-service facilities. Runtime services are a different interface with their own preservation and mapping requirements. Keeping a pointer to the old boot-services table does not keep those services available.',
    ], 'Handle an invalidated map key', 'The loader has loaded the kernel and prepared handoff storage. It obtains a memory map with key K1, but an intervening permitted action changes the firmware map.', [
      ['Attempt the handoff', 'Call ExitBootServices with K1. The firmware reports that the key is invalid. Do not proceed as if boot services had ended successfully, and do not reuse the rejected map as final evidence.', 'K1 rejected'],
      ['Refresh through the retry path', 'Use the restricted recovery path permitted after the failed exit attempt to obtain the current map and its new key. Avoid unrelated printing, allocations, or protocol work that could invalidate the map again or violate post-attempt restrictions.', 'new key = K2'],
      ['Exit and transfer control', 'Retry with the fresh key. Once successful, pass the validated boot information to the kernel and use only the interfaces allowed after that transition. Preserve required firmware and handoff regions in the initial memory ownership plan.', 'boot services unavailable'],
    ], 'The map and key belong to one observation; success is the boundary that changes which services exist.', 'The map-buffer allocation changes the map', 'A first size query followed by allocating exactly that amount can be insufficient because the allocation itself may add descriptors. Reserve headroom and handle the documented buffer-too-small result by acquiring a sufficiently large buffer.', 'Can the loader print through the boot console after a successful exit? No. Record diagnostics before the final handoff or use a kernel-owned output path afterward; retaining the old function pointer does not preserve the service.'),
    syscalls: lesson('Fast entry leaves more state management to the stub', [
      'A fast system-call instruction reduces part of the transition machinery but does not construct the complete saved frame used by the earlier interrupt path. In 64-bit SYSCALL entry, software must establish a trusted stack and save the return state it will need. Treat the first few instructions as a carefully audited boundary before ordinary C can run.',
      'The return address and flags remain untrusted state to validate under the kernel’s return policy. A permitted user destination must satisfy both the selected canonical-address rule and the kernel’s user-range policy. Stack addresses, mappings, privilege state, and permitted flags also belong to the complete return proof.',
    ], 'Trace state that must survive one syscall', 'A user program requests a service while its RSP names its user stack. The CPU transfers to the configured 64-bit syscall entry.', [
      ['Capture the architectural return state', 'SYSCALL places the return instruction address and flags in its defined registers, including RCX and R11 for this path. Save them before code that may overwrite them. The user wrapper must also describe the relevant clobbers to its compiler.', 'return state needs explicit storage'],
      ['Select trusted storage', 'Save the user stack pointer using the entry design’s safe per-CPU mechanism and install a kernel stack before pushing a C-call frame. Unlike the earlier privilege-changing interrupt gate path, do not assume SYSCALL loaded a TSS stack for you.', 'switch stack in software'],
      ['Validate the exit path', 'Dispatch the service, then check the saved destination and flags according to the chosen return mechanism. Use a correct slow return or failure path for states unsuitable for the fast return; a shortcut must not bypass validation.', 'validated return before user execution'],
    ], 'Fewer automatic entry actions mean more explicit software obligations.', 'Switch to trusted stack storage', 'A malicious application can choose an unmapped or supervisor-pointing stack value. Any early push before selecting trusted storage can fault or corrupt state at the most fragile point of entry.', 'Does a lower-half canonical RIP prove safe return? No. It only passes an address-shape or range policy. The complete implementation must also establish the required mapping, permissions, flags, and stack state.'),
    migration: lesson('Attach evidence to each architectural milestone', [
      'A migration plan should preserve a working reference and make each new stage independently observable. Each serial marker should identify the state just established: long-mode code reached, new stack installed, interrupt delivered, or user program resumed.',
      'Use several memory arrangements before declaring address handling complete. A physical allocation accidentally truncated to 32 bits may remain invisible when every frame is low. A hard-coded identity map may appear correct until the loader places a module elsewhere. Vary only supported configurations and preserve the exact failing command.',
    ], 'Stage the port without combining every new feature', 'Keep the existing IA-32 branch bootable. Build a native QEMU branch that first establishes 64-bit kernel execution, then adds firmware and user-entry work in separate milestones.', [
      ['Prove minimal execution', 'Reach a 64-bit entry marker with a known stack and a controlled page fault diagnostic. Inspect the active instruction mode and mappings to establish which environment actually executed.', 'milestone: kernel entry'],
      ['Prove the firmware handoff', 'Add the UEFI loader contract and demonstrate successful exit from boot services with preserved boot information. Keep the earlier kernel marker so failures before and after the handoff remain distinguishable.', 'milestone: handoff accepted'],
      ['Restore higher-level behavior', 'Bring up timer handling, allocation, and eventually a user process one subsystem at a time. For each, record a valid trace and a deliberate failure, such as an unmapped user return target or exhausted allocation.', 'milestone: isolated user execution'],
    ], 'A sequence of specific passing claims is easier to debug than one enormous port.', 'Changing three boundaries obscures the first broken one', 'Replacing firmware, paging mode, and scheduler together makes a missing boot marker ambiguous. Retain intermediate configurations and commands so each transition has a known predecessor.', 'If the browser return-range checkpoint passes but native QEMU faults at the mode switch, what does the pass establish? Only the tested helper’s arithmetic policy. Investigate the native transition state and mappings as a separate execution claim.'),
  },
});

Object.assign(systemsDepth, {
  'fat-read-driver': {
    compare: lesson('Follow the allocation-table links', [
      'A file’s logical byte order is independent of the numerical order of its clusters. The directory supplies the starting point and length; the table supplies the route. This is why a reader that merely increments the cluster number can pass tests on a fresh image but fail after files have been created and deleted.',
      'Keep allocation size separate from file size. A final cluster may be only partly used, but the entire cluster belongs to the file’s allocation chain. Reading all allocated bytes would expose padding beyond EOF. The directory length limits the visible byte stream even when the chain contains enough storage for more.',
    ], 'Read a fragmented three-cluster file', 'Clusters hold 2,048 bytes. A file has size 5,000 bytes and chain 9 -> 4 -> 17 -> end.', [
      ['Locate the first piece', 'The directory gives cluster 9. Its first 2,048 bytes become file offsets 0 through 2,047. The next physical cluster, 10, has no implied relationship to this file.', 'remaining = 2952 bytes'],
      ['Follow the table', 'Decode the link from 9 to 4 and validate it before reading cluster 4. That cluster supplies file offsets 2,048 through 4,095. Its lower number does not mean the reader moved backward in the file.', 'remaining = 904 bytes'],
      ['Trim the final piece', 'Cluster 17 supplies only 904 visible bytes. The remaining 1,144 bytes of that allocated cluster lie beyond the file size and must not be returned as content.', 'allocated 6144; visible 5000'],
    ], 'The chain establishes location; the size establishes how much of that location is meaningful.', 'Derive the FAT type from cluster count', 'A formatted volume may contain a human-readable FAT label, but the derived cluster count determines which entry representation the driver must use. Choosing by a string can make every later link decode wrong.', 'What if the FAT link from cluster 9 points directly to end? The declared 5,000-byte file cannot fit in its first 2,048-byte cluster. Report inconsistent metadata when the chain cannot supply the declared file length.'),
    geometry: lesson('Derive every region from checked quantities', [
      'Volume geometry is a dependency graph. Bytes per sector affect root-directory rounding; FAT count and FAT size affect the start of data; the remaining data sectors determine cluster count; cluster count determines the FAT family and legal data-cluster range. A wrong early field contaminates every later address unless it is rejected first.',
      'Use wide, checked intermediate arithmetic and verify each region fits inside the volume before subtracting. A malformed metadata area larger than the volume can otherwise underflow into an enormous data region. Also verify that the FAT has enough entries to describe the derived data clusters; the table must have capacity for every derived data cluster and its reserved entries.',
    ], 'Compute a small FAT16 layout', 'Assume 512-byte sectors, one reserved sector, two FATs of 32 sectors each, 512 fixed root entries, 32,865 total sectors, and four sectors per cluster.', [
      ['Round the fixed root', 'Each entry occupies 32 bytes, so 512 entries need 16,384 bytes. Dividing by 512 gives exactly 32 root-directory sectors; no partial final sector needs rounding here.', 'root sectors = 32'],
      ['Find data and type', 'The first data sector is 1 + 2 × 32 + 32 = 97. There are 32,768 data sectors, producing 8,192 data clusters. This count lies in the FAT16 range, and each FAT has capacity for 8,192 sixteen-bit slots only.', 'data clusters = 8192'],
      ['Reject the hidden inconsistency', 'The table also needs entries 0 and 1, so 8,192 slots cannot describe 8,192 data clusters plus the two reserved entries. Reject this deliberately inconsistent BPB because its table capacity is insufficient.', 'required slots = 8194'],
    ], 'Correct formulas can still describe an impossible volume; cross-check their results.', 'Validate every derived geometry bound', 'A reader that calculates the data start but ignores FAT capacity can later read links beyond the table. Check both region placement and whether the metadata can represent the objects it claims to manage.', 'How could the fixture be repaired? Increase the FAT capacity and recompute the resulting first-data and cluster counts, or reduce the data region. Changing one BPB field requires repeating dependent calculations.'),
    decode: lesson('Decode packed fields with a byte window', [
      'Packed on-disk formats rarely align with C object boundaries. Decode from unsigned bytes after checking the whole input span. This avoids alignment assumptions, host-endian dependence, and signed-char promotion surprises. An in-memory decoder should be independently testable with a tiny byte array before it is connected to a sector cache.',
      'A two-byte window may cross a sector boundary even when the table entry itself is only twelve bits wide. The disk-facing layer must obtain both bytes successfully, keeping neither a stale cache pointer nor a half-initialized scratch value after failure. The bit arithmetic begins only after that storage contract is satisfied.',
    ], 'Decode an odd FAT12 entry', 'Take cluster entry 343. Its FAT byte offset is floor(3 × 343 / 2), or 514. The two bytes at offsets 514 and 515 are 0xb7 and 0x6c.', [
      ['Build an unsigned window', 'Little-endian assembly gives 0x6cb7: 0xb7 is the low byte and 0x6c is shifted by eight bits. No cast to an unaligned uint16_t pointer is required.', 'window = 0x6cb7'],
      ['Choose the owned bits', 'Entry 343 is odd, so its twelve bits occupy the high part of this window. Shift right by four to obtain 0x6cb. The low nibble, 7, belongs to its neighbor and is excluded.', 'decoded entry = 0x6cb'],
      ['Interpret the result', 'Validate the decoded numeric link against marker rules and the volume’s cluster bounds. The chain walker must classify free, reserved, bad, end, and usable link values for the selected FAT family and volume.', 'decode precedes link validation'],
    ], 'Byte availability, bit extraction, and semantic classification are three separate checks.', 'Correct extraction can still read out of bounds', 'Masking a value after an invalid two-byte load does not repair the access. Check that offset and width fit the available byte array before constructing the window.', 'How does entry 342 differ with the same window position? Its even entry begins at offset 513, so it uses a different overlapping two-byte window and takes its low twelve bits. Parity changes both the offset relation and extraction rule.'),
    directories: lesson('Parse directory records before interpreting names', [
      'A directory sector is a sequence of fixed-width records with several possible meanings. The first byte and attributes classify a record before its remaining fields are interpreted. Classify long-name fragments and deleted slots before interpreting their remaining fields; only suitable live records supply a file chain.',
      'Convert an accepted eleven-byte short name into a separately bounded, terminated display string. The on-disk base and extension are space-padded fields without a required C terminator. Path traversal adds another layer: each intermediate component must resolve to a directory under the implementation’s supported naming rules.',
    ], 'Walk a sector containing several record types', 'A directory block contains a deleted slot, a volume label, a normal short-name file, and then a record whose first byte is zero.', [
      ['Classify before following pointers', 'Skip the deleted slot and volume label for an ordinary file lookup. Their remaining bytes may resemble cluster numbers, but the classification says they are not normal file contents to traverse.', 'no chain followed yet'],
      ['Decode the normal file', 'Read its bounded name fields, attributes, first cluster, and byte size. A zero-length regular file can be represented without a data chain. A nonempty file must pass the appropriate first-cluster checks.', 'file metadata validated'],
      ['Honor the end marker', 'The zero first byte marks the end of used directory entries. Stop at the end marker, leaving later stale slots outside the active directory. A deleted marker means skip one slot; an end marker means terminate this enumeration.', 'end used entries'],
    ], 'Record classification protects both names and the addresses derived from them.', 'Use the directory format’s traversal bound', 'The rules for the fixed FAT12/16 root and cluster-chain directories differ. Use the region or chain traversal bound specified for that directory format.', 'Why can a path component that looks like a filename still fail traversal? Its record may be an ordinary file when a directory is required, or its chain may be malformed. Name matching alone does not establish the next object’s type.'),
    read: lesson('Advance through the file in bounded spans', [
      'A read has three simultaneous limits: bytes requested by the caller, bytes remaining before EOF, and bytes remaining in the current cluster. Copy the minimum of those limits, then advance the logical file position. Only when the cluster’s available span is exhausted should the walker decode and validate another link.',
      'The traversal itself also needs a bound independent of the requested byte count. A malicious chain can cycle while still producing enough repeated bytes to satisfy a short request. A visited set detects repetition explicitly; a step budget guarantees eventual termination. Decide and document which corruption detection guarantees your first reader provides.',
    ], 'Read across a fragmented boundary', 'Clusters hold 1,024 bytes. A 2,500-byte file follows 6 -> 12 -> 7 -> end. Read 1,700 bytes starting at file offset 900.', [
      ['Trim to the visible file', 'Only 2,500 minus 900, or 1,600 bytes, remain before EOF. The request must therefore return at most 1,600 bytes even though the caller supplied capacity for 1,700.', 'visible request = 1600'],
      ['Finish the first cluster', 'Offset 900 lies in cluster 6, leaving 124 bytes there. Copy those bytes, validate the link to 12, and continue with 1,476 bytes still needed.', 'copied = 124'],
      ['Complete the remainder', 'Copy 1,024 bytes from cluster 12 and 452 from cluster 7. The three pieces total 1,600. Stop at EOF without returning the unused tail of the final cluster.', '124 + 1024 + 452 = 1600'],
    ], 'Every copy length follows from the intersection of caller, file, and allocation boundaries.', 'Compare chain capacity with declared length', 'The directory size already declared how many bytes exist. If the chain ends before those bytes can be reached, report corruption or the documented partial-error result while retaining the declared file size.', 'What if the request begins at offset 2,500? Return EOF without reading a data cluster. The exclusive file endpoint is a valid position at which zero content bytes remain.'),
    matrix: lesson('Build fixtures that distinguish plausible wrong readers', [
      'A test fixture should be selected because it defeats a specific shortcut. A contiguous file cannot distinguish a chain walker from a reader that increments clusters. A whole-cluster file cannot distinguish correct EOF trimming from copying every allocated byte. Write the wrong implementation you want the fixture to expose in the test description.',
      'Independent tools provide a second implementation of the format. Create or inspect an image with a known external formatter and reader, then compare the exact returned contents and length. Preserve the image and its hash so a later change to the fixture cannot silently change the meaning of an old test result.',
    ], 'Assemble a compact reader acceptance set', 'Use three files whose lengths are zero, one byte, and one byte more than a cluster. Place the last file on a deliberately fragmented chain.', [
      ['Exercise the empty path', 'The zero-length file should return EOF without demanding a usable data-cluster chain. Record the block requests to confirm that no accidental payload read occurs.', 'payload reads = 0'],
      ['Exercise the visible tail', 'The one-byte file should return exactly its known byte. Fill the unused cluster remainder with a different pattern so copying padding becomes immediately visible.', 'returned length = 1'],
      ['Exercise link following', 'The larger file must read its final byte from the second cluster identified by the FAT link. Corrupt that link in a separate fixture and require a bounded classified failure.', 'second cluster determined by FAT'],
    ], 'These deliberately chosen lengths exercise distinct allocation, boundary, and link-following cases.', 'Do not mutate the only reference image', 'Keep valid fixtures immutable and create named corrupted copies. Otherwise a previous test’s mutation may change the input to the next test and obscure the cause of its result.', 'What extra fixture tests the storage boundary inside FAT12 decoding? Choose a link whose two-byte window straddles a sector boundary, then independently fail the second sector read and verify the parser never publishes a decoded value.'),
  },
  'fat-write-driver': {
    contract: lesson('State what a successful write promises after a crash', [
      'A write API has at least three observable moments: the caller’s bytes enter software-owned memory, a later reader can discover them, and the storage contract says they survive loss of volatile state. These moments can be different. Naming them prevents a successful cache update from being described as durable persistence.',
      'Begin with an intentionally small mutation set such as creating and appending short-name regular files under a volume mutex. This narrows the number of intermediate states. It does not remove crash states: a single writer can still be interrupted between two sector updates, and a mutex cannot survive a power cut to finish the operation.',
    ], 'Describe a create operation from two observers', 'A process creates a 700-byte file on a volume with 512-byte clusters. Another reader searches the directory, while a test harness can discard volatile writes at selected points.', [
      ['Reserve and prepare', 'The writer reserves two clusters and fills them with the intended data, including a defined policy for unused tail bytes. The directory still does not expose the new file, so a concurrent logical lookup cannot yet discover it.', 'candidate allocation = 2 clusters'],
      ['Publish visibility', 'After data and chain preparation under the chosen ordering policy, the writer installs the directory record. Readers can now resolve the name and size. The publication step makes the prepared data reachable by readers.', 'directory size = 700'],
      ['Establish the promised persistence', 'Perform the flush operations required by the block interface before reporting any durability guarantee. The crash harness must model which issued writes became durable under those guarantees.', 'durable only after required flush'],
    ], 'A truthful API describes both when bytes become visible and which completed operation makes them persistent.', 'Separate update exclusion from crash recovery', 'Exclusive access prevents concurrent software updates from interleaving. It cannot make several independent disk writes atomic, and it cannot roll back a device that loses power halfway through a metadata update.', 'Can close merely release the descriptor? Yes, if that is the documented API, but then callers must use a separate durability operation when required. Tests should verify the exact persistence promise made by the chosen interface.'),
    entries: lesson('Preserve the neighboring bits during an update', [
      'A packed table entry occupies bits that can share storage bytes with neighboring entries. Updating it requires reading the containing bytes, preserving bits outside the owned field, and combining the new value. The preserving mask is as important as the new link value because another file may own the adjacent entry.',
      'The read-modify-write must also be protected at the cache or volume level. Two callers can each compute a correct byte window from the same old sector and still lose one update when they write their separate copies back. Field correctness and concurrent ownership are independent proofs.',
    ], 'Replace one even FAT12 entry', 'A two-byte window is 0xd234. Its low twelve bits belong to an even FAT12 entry; the upper nibble belongs to the adjacent entry. Set the selected entry to 0x5a6.', [
      ['Identify preserved bits', 'Mask the old window with 0xf000 to retain 0xd000. The old selected value 0x234 is discarded. Do not clear the high nibble simply because the new link uses only twelve bits.', 'preserved = 0xd000'],
      ['Combine the new field', 'Validate or mask the selected link according to the helper’s interface, then combine 0xd000 with 0x5a6. The resulting window is 0xd5a6, stored little-endian as bytes 0xa6 and 0xd5.', 'new window = 0xd5a6'],
      ['Check the neighbor', 'Decode the adjacent field before and after the update and require it to match. Repeat with an odd selected entry and with a window spanning two sectors to test both ownership masks and storage behavior.', 'neighbor nibble remains 0xd'],
    ], 'A correct mutation changes exactly the intended field and nothing outside it.', 'Two-sector updates can tear', 'Even with correct in-memory masking, a FAT12 window crossing a sector boundary may be only partly persisted after a failure. The writer must not claim atomic on-disk updates merely because the helper returned success.', 'For an odd entry, which bits remain untouched? Preserve the low four bits of the window and replace the high twelve. Reusing the even-entry mask would corrupt the neighbor and misplace the new value.'),
    allocate: lesson('Make free-space search finite and ownership explicit', [
      'A next-free hint suggests where to start searching; reserving a cluster requires a separate ownership update. The allocator must inspect the authoritative FAT entry while holding the volume’s allocation protection. After selecting a free cluster, it must mark or reserve it before another allocator can select the same one.',
      'Bound the search by the usable cluster population and visit at most one full cycle. This makes a full disk an ordinary outcome with known work. Validate the hint first; unknown, reserved, or out-of-range values should fall back to a legal starting point without changing the correctness of the search.',
    ], 'Search a small volume with a stale hint', 'The simplified volume has usable clusters 2 through 7. The hint is 5. Entries 5, 6, and 7 are allocated, entry 2 is free, and entries 3 and 4 are allocated.', [
      ['Start from the hint', 'Inspect cluster 5, then 6, then 7 under the allocation protocol. Each allocated value means continue; the hint’s presence does not entitle the caller to overwrite cluster 5.', 'visited: 5, 6, 7'],
      ['Wrap once', 'Continue at cluster 2 and observe its free marker. Reserve it before releasing protection. Publish the reservation or final allocated state before a later caller can claim the same cluster.', 'selected = 2'],
      ['Update derived information', 'Set the next search hint to a suitable later candidate and adjust any maintained free count according to its validity. If writing optional hint metadata fails, report or recover according to policy without treating the chosen cluster as free again accidentally.', 'FAT remains allocation truth'],
    ], 'The hint improves search order; the protected authoritative update grants ownership.', 'A free count can be stale in either direction', 'A count claiming free space does not identify a free cluster. A count claiming zero should not be blindly trusted if it is only a rebuildable hint. Define when counts are verified and when a scan is required.', 'If cluster 2 were allocated too, how long should the search continue? Inspect 3 and 4, then report full after six candidates total. Reaching the hint again must not begin an unbounded second pass.'),
    ordering: lesson('Prefer recoverable leftovers to visible uninitialized data', [
      'Write ordering is a dependency problem. A visible directory entry depends on a usable chain, and that chain depends on initialized data. Publishing a parent reference before preparing its target creates a state where ordinary readers can reach incomplete storage. Reversing that order may leave unreachable allocations after a crash, which are still a problem but can be identified as recovery work.',
      'Do not describe ordering alone as full atomicity. A crash can occur inside an individual metadata update, redundant FAT copies can disagree, and device persistence behavior matters. State the simplified block guarantees under which your experiment runs, then report every remaining inconsistent or leaked state honestly.',
    ], 'Create a file with two reserved clusters', 'A new 900-byte file needs clusters 21 and 8 on a volume with 512-byte clusters. Neither cluster is visible from any directory before the operation.', [
      ['Prepare payload', 'Write the first 512 bytes to cluster 21 and the remaining 388 to cluster 8. Complete the data persistence step required by the chosen interface before exposing a chain that readers may follow.', 'data pieces = 512 + 388'],
      ['Prepare the route', 'Install 21 -> 8 and mark 8 as end-of-chain under the FAT update policy, then perform the required metadata persistence step. A crash now can leave an allocated but unreachable chain.', 'chain exists; name absent'],
      ['Publish the name', 'Create the short directory entry with first cluster 21 and size 900, then persist that directory update. Only this stage makes the complete candidate reachable by an ordinary filename lookup.', 'name -> cluster 21; size = 900'],
    ], 'Every publication points to dependencies prepared earlier, while recovery accounts for unpublished leftovers.', 'Appending has an existing visible owner', 'Extending an old chain changes metadata that readers can already reach, so its ordering needs a separate proof. Prepare new storage, connect it using the chosen ordering, and avoid increasing the visible size before the chain can provide those bytes.', 'If a crash happens after the chain is durable but before the directory entry, should a checker report a healthy named file? No. It may find an orphaned allocation requiring recovery, but no directory entry yet gives it that file identity.'),
    lfn: lesson('Treat a long name as an uncommitted candidate', [
      'Long-name records are fragments associated with a later short entry. Keep fragments as pending metadata until their sequence and association checks establish a complete filename. Reset the candidate whenever the sequence breaks so fragments from an old or damaged entry cannot spill into a new file’s identity.',
      'Bound accumulation before conversion. A limit on fragments controls input work, a limit on UTF-16 code units controls intermediate storage, and a limit on encoded output bytes controls the display or lookup buffer. These limits differ because one Unicode character can require multiple code units or output bytes.',
    ], 'Reject a name assembled from unrelated fragments', 'The parser sees fragments indicating a three-part long name, but their recorded order is 3, then 1, then the short entry. The expected middle fragment is missing.', [
      ['Begin a candidate', 'The first fragment establishes the declared sequence extent and expected next ordinal. Store only within the allowed fragment budget and record the association checksum for later comparison.', 'expected next fragment = 2'],
      ['Detect the gap', 'The next fragment has ordinal 1; the sequence requires ordinal 2. Discard the accumulated candidate immediately. Continuing would fabricate a name by treating missing characters as padding or stale memory.', 'candidate invalidated'],
      ['Handle the short entry', 'Parse the short entry independently. Under a fallback policy, expose its valid short alias without attaching the broken long name. If a complete valid sequence existed, its checksum and Unicode conversion would still need validation.', 'no long name attached'],
    ], 'Association with the short entry is a commit point for a previously tentative name.', 'Validate sequence and capacity alongside checksum', 'Matching the short-name checksum does not excuse missing ordinals, excessive fragments, invalid code-unit sequences, or output overflow. Each structural condition protects a different part of the parser.', 'Why must an empty or deleted record clear a pending candidate? It breaks the adjacency and sequence that associates those fragments with a following short entry. Retaining them could rename an unrelated file accidentally.'),
    crash: lesson('Test each point where a write can be interrupted', [
      'A deterministic crash harness should separate writes accepted into volatile state from writes made durable by the promised flush semantics. At each selected cut point, discard the volatile portion and reconstruct the device from the durable snapshot. Otherwise the test silently assumes persistence that the writer never requested.',
      'Use an independent checker and reader after each simulated restart. Your own reader can agree with your writer because both share the same mistake. Classify results: old state preserved, new state complete, leaked allocation, inconsistent metadata, or data mismatch. A classification is more informative than a single pass/fail label during development.',
    ], 'Inspect three cuts in one create operation', 'Create a 1,200-byte file from an unchanged base image. Run separate trials cutting after data persistence, after FAT-chain persistence, and after directory persistence.', [
      ['Cut after data', 'The payload bytes may exist on disk, but the new file has no published directory record. Determine whether reservations were persisted and whether any allocations need recovery; do not infer a named file from recognizable raw bytes.', 'name should be absent'],
      ['Cut after chain', 'The chain may be allocated and durable while still unreachable. Compare allocated clusters with clusters reachable from all valid directories. Report any orphan as a recovery issue in the operation’s result.', 'possible orphaned chain'],
      ['Cut after directory', 'The complete file should be discoverable under the stated persistence assumptions. Compare its exact 1,200 bytes and inspect redundant metadata copies. A matching filename alone does not prove the content survived.', 'expected named content = 1200 bytes'],
    ], 'Crash testing evaluates intermediate states that a normal round-trip never observes.', 'Restart each trial from the same baseline', 'Reusing yesterday’s partially mutated image changes allocation choices and crash consequences. Reconstruct the original image before each cut so differences are caused by the chosen interruption point.', 'What extra trial targets a FAT12 entry across sectors? Cut between the two sector writes. This exposes torn field persistence even when the higher-level create ordering and in-memory masking are correct.'),
  },
});

const flow = (title, intro, nodes, edges, caption) => ({
  title, intro,
  nodes: nodes.map(([id, label, detail, kind = 'process']) => ({ id, label, detail, kind })),
  edges: edges.map(([from, to, label]) => ({ from, to, ...(label ? { label } : {}) })),
  caption,
});

